From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Sun, 17 Jul 1994 16:39:08 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-148] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

Michael Clive Price writes:
>You had said that you assumed:
>> .... the number of valuable chunks one discovers per unit of internal
>> processing time is proportional to the number of chunks one already
>> has .....
>A blank, simple net should never learn anything by this logic.  Yet they
>do.  So why should I believe that this assumption, plucked from nowhere
>as far as I can see, applies to more complex things, and complex neural
>nets in particular, when it doesn't apply to simple nets?

No net starts totally "blank".  Most any neural net researcher will
tell you that much of their application success comes from carefully
representing the data to input, and structuring the neural net to
match.  So representation knowledge is included.  Also, it is natural
to think of the basic "local learning rules" as embodying at least a
few knowledge chunks.

The concept of knowledge chucks was created to help explain cognitive
science data on real humans (running on real neural nets).  The idea
that people who know more about some area can discover things about it
faster is also well supported by data on people.  

>Even so you seemed to be making the assumption that the neural nets have
>to discover everything for themselves.  Why can't they be trained like
>humans?  I see no reason why a human-level neural net running on a
>hardware platform of approx 10^17 bits/sec should take any longer than
>a 10^17 bits/sec human to learn about the world.  

Babies do *not* enter the world as blank nueral slates.  They are born
with an incredible amount of knowledge which allows them to quickly
learn to communicate with us, and to catch on to the nuances of
current human culture.  Ask yourself why you can't teach all we know
to whales, with much larger brains than us.  (I first learned of this
stuff from a book "Programmed to Learn".)

>> [Is] it obvious that you will find the right local neural net learning
>> rule before this?
>
>No, but I'd bet and speculate on it.  In a sense I have bet on it, since
>I'm signed for cryonics.

How is cryonics related to whether AI happens before uploads?

>I view logic, language and symbol manipulation as mere epiphenomena of
>neural activity and trying to model human symbol manipulation directly
>is a mistake.  

Should we view a bird's ability to fly, and the pressure gradients
near their wings, as mere epiphenomena of bird feathers, so that
trying to reproduce similar pressure gradients without feathers is a
mistake?

Robin Hanson

