From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Date: Sun, 17 Jul 1994 19:17:07 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-149] If Knowledge Limits Learning

Mike Price says:
>Even so you seemed to be making the assumption that the neural nets have
>to discover everything for themselves.  Why can't they be trained like
>humans?  I see no reason why a human-level neural net running on a
>hardware platform of approx 10^17 bits/sec should take any longer than
>a 10^17 bits/sec human to learn about the world.

I think the neural net could learn about the world at the same rate as the
human if and only if it had the same level of feedback, and the same
ability to affect its environment in ways that are meaningful to it, that
the human has.

Seeing and learning about seeing are not the same thing.  Similarly for
hearing, tasting, balancing, lifting, and emoting.  Without the ability to
interact with the world, the net will learn, not about the world, but about
what humans have encoded about the world.

Mind you, even if it could interact in such a way, the net would not learn
the same things about the world that a human would.  Humans learn that fire
(and steam) can burn, that spray-painting in a closed room can cause
lightheadedness, that unrequited love can cause emotional upset.  There is
no reason for a net interacting with the world using mechanical sensors and
actuators to learn any of this -- and that's just the tip of the iceberg.
Consequently, much of what the net learns about the world may not be useful
to humans (but may make for interesting study nonetheless, if we can
understand it).

Kennita


