Structuring the net means choosing the number of hidden layers, nodes
per layer etc.  You seem to be talking about brain architectures,
whereas I am talking about the connection strengths, which are usually
randomised at start, which seems the more relevant point.  The types of
knowledge contained in the architecture and the knowledge contained in
the more dynamic connections are probably completely different and
inequivalent.  It could be very dangerous to draw an analogy between
them.

> So representation knowledge is included.  

> Also, it is natural to think of the basic "local learning rules" 
> as embodying at least a few knowledge chunks.

No, I don't see this at all.  Since I'm guessing that the same "local
learning rules" will operate on all scale networks then they contain no
knowledge about the environment or any species-specific information.

> The concept of knowledge chucks was created to help explain cognitive
> science data on real humans (running on real neural nets).  The idea
> that people who know more about some area can discover things about
> it faster is also well supported by data on people.

So why do people learn faster when younger?  I was under the impression
that learning rates drop with age.  Look at language acquisition, for
instance.  Back propagation work fastest at the beginning, as well.

>> Even so you seemed to be making the assumption that the neural 
>> nets have to discover everything for themselves. [...]
>
> Babies do *not* enter the world as blank nueral slates.  [...]

This is a different issue, and one which I don't take issue with.

Digression: I suspect that a lot of the genetic pre-programming you're
talking about is associated with the learning rules, eg pleasure/pain
structures to interpret, say, a smack as negative reinforcement and a
parental smile as positive.  However it would be a mistake to look at
the brain structures at birth and assume that it is *all* genetically
hardwired in.  Much learning takes place in the womb, explicable within
the connectionist model.  Many of the brain architecture, major pathways
etc, present at birth may have developed this way, following the
positioning of nerve inputs.  This has been demonstrated for visual
pathways in other animals (coordinated firing of retinal cells =>
prenatal pathways in the visual cortex) and is probably true for other
areas of the cortex and for humans.  (Whilst talking about babies, BTW,
it is noteworthy that the way that human babies acquire early motor co-
ordination (arm movements) has been modeled on neural networks.)

This is all a digression, though, and does not address my point that
your learning time figures assume no externally driven training.  Yes,
perhaps it would take an UIM two hundred thousand years to rediscover 
algebra and the law of gravity, if we rely entirely on independent 
rediscovery.  I have no problem with this, I just don't see its 
relevance.  Newton did his orginal work in decades by "standing on the 
shoulders of ye Giants", why can't a UIM do the same?

