From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Mon, 18 Jul 1994 07:48:09 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-7-154] If Knowledge Limits Learning

Mike Price replies to Robin Hanson:

> > No net starts totally "blank".

> Structuring the net means choosing the number of hidden layers, nodes
> per layer etc.  You seem to be talking about brain architectures,
> whereas I am talking about the connection strengths, which are usually
> randomised at start, which seems the more relevant point.

Consider a matrix representing connection strengths between all
neurons in a net.  Selecting an architecture constrains some entries
in this matrix to a non-random value -- zero.  Specifying this
"starting data" makes the slate non-blank.  The important question
is how much information (however encoded) has to be contained on
the slate intially for learning to add the rest.

I think the amount is likely to be rather large (looking for
clues to the information content of DNA codings of protiens devoted
to constructing our nervous systems).

On a related note, I always thought Lenat's CYC project, the exact
opposite of Mike's hope for a seed AI that grows into a mind flower
under the influence of the environment, was a bit strange.  They hope
that once a certain critical mass of information is entered that the
system will be able to learn more by itself.  But it always seemed to
me that most of the information they actually enter has nothing
plausible to do with requirements for learning... that information is
demonstrably not necessary; and why should we believe it's sufficient?

> > Also, it is natural to think of the basic "local learning rules" 
> > as embodying at least a few knowledge chunks.
> 
> No, I don't see this at all.  Since I'm guessing that the same "local
> learning rules" will operate on all scale networks then they contain no
> knowledge about the environment or any species-specific information.

A local learning rule that performs gradient descent or "distance"-based
clustering, or whatever, contains a priori the very useful "knowledge"
that such actions are likely to be useful.  A true tabula rasa wouldn't
be able to estimate an error gradient, much less know whether moving up
or down or sideways was a good plan.

derek


