From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 18 Jul 1994 09:37:21 -0700
Subject: [#94-7-157] If Knowledge Limits Learning

Derek Zahn writes:
>I always thought Lenat's CYC project ... hope[s] that once a certain
>critical mass of information is entered that the system will be able
>to learn more by itself.  But it always seemed to me that most of the
>information they actually enter has nothing plausible to do with
>requirements for learning

Actually, Lenat would say he already knows how to make CYC learn, and
has demonstrated this with EURISKO.  He thinks the learning *rate*,
however, is limited by the amount of knowledge a system already
contains.  Hence the focus of CYC, and the title of this thread.

Mike Price originally wrote:
>My hope is that with some as yet unknown local learning rule that the
>right kind of complexity will emerge as a spontaneous order, within
>the small randomly connected network as it develops into a bigger
>"intelligently connected" network. ...  The limiting thing is
>searching through the learning algorithm space.  ...  which is what I
>mean by AI research being hardware limited, rather than software limited.

Now Mike seems to have a bit different vision:
>your learning time figures assume no externally driven training.  Yes,
>perhaps it would take an UIM two hundred thousand years to rediscover 
>algebra and the law of gravity, if we rely entirely on independent 
>rediscovery.  I have no problem with this, I just don't see its 
>relevance.  Newton did his orginal work in decades by "standing on the 
>shoulders of ye Giants", why can't a UIM do the same?

The question is now: How much does a neural net or any potentially
intelligent system need to know to be "trained" by us, to learn the
way human children do, by watching, listening to, and talking with
human parents, teachers, etc. (and by listening in the womb).  The
evidence seems to me pretty strong that the answer is "a whole lot".

Consider how hard it is for us to "train" whales or elephants, who
have bigger brains than us, and share a great deal of common genes.
Clearly human babies know something they don't.  In learning language,
for example, humans seem to start with strong expectations about
language should be like, rather than being open to a wider range of
possibilities.  Humans seem "programmed to learn" language at an early
age.  (And language acquisition rates naturally drop with age, as Mike
mentions.)

As Mike mentions, developing brains take many clues from their inputs,
such as visual and motor control, in forming neural structures.  This
makes sense if we think of our genetic inheritance as informationally
compressed; if genes can find a way to encode some final structure by
saying "look here then for that info", instead of directly encoding
that info, they will do so.  Similar things happen with encoding
non-brain body structures.  This does not mean there isn't a great
deal of knowledge/info encoded in the generative system, any more than
most developing animal bodies are designed on the fly from a few
general animal body rules.

If a net must know a great deal before it can be trained by humans,
then the crucial question is where will this knowledge come from?  If
each part, such as the trick to learn some motor control Mike
mentions being recently learned, must be directly entered by neural
net researchers, this isn't obviously a faster approach than CYC. 

>Are you saying that you don't believe neural nets have anything to
>offer or are you saying that they do, but that you still believe that
>generalised learning can be modelled algorithmically as well?

I'm saying both approaches seem not obviously unreasonable to me.

Robin Hanson

